course: dictionary
lesson: 9941
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Context, the words and sentences around a word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «A tariff is a tax on things brought in from abroad» shows a tariff is money paid on goods from other countries.
explain: In a price list, tariff means something different. A UK phone or energy tariff is a list of charges, such as the cost of each unit of electricity. Read the clues: if the sentence is about money paid on goods, it is a tax. If it is about a choice of phone or energy plan, it is a price list.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «It was reported that the USA put tariffs on goods from other countries» makes sense with tax. Try list of prices there, and it does not fit. This quick test helps you choose the right meaning.
fact: A tariff is a tax that a country makes people pay on goods brought in from another country.
fact: In the UK, a tariff can also be a list of prices or charges, for example for electricity, phone calls, hotel rooms or trains.
fact: «A 10% tariff means £1 on every £10, so a £10 toy costs £11 to bring in.»
fact: «Some say tariffs protect home factories and bring jobs back; others say the cost is often passed on to shoppers as higher prices.»
fact: Tariffs are a topic in social studies and news, and in maths a tariff can be a percentage added to a price.
exercise: «A tariff is a tax on things brought in from abroad.» What is a tariff? | A list of holiday dates | *A tax on things brought in from abroad | A gift given to a friend | A factory that makes cars
exercise: «Some say tariffs protect jobs at home, others say they make things dearer for shoppers.» What does dearer mean? | Cheaper | Further away | Kinder | *More expensive
exercise: Mia's family compares phone tariffs before they choose one. What does tariff mean in this sentence? | A type of bird | A sports ground | A tax on imported cars | *A list of prices or charges
test: «A 10% tariff means £1 on every £10, so a £10 toy costs £11 to bring in.» How much does a £10 toy cost after a 10% tariff? | *£11 | £12 | £1 | £10
test: Which meaning fits: a list of prices for electricity or phone calls? | A kind of tool | A tax on goods from abroad | *A tariff | A rule for football
exam: «Some say tariffs protect home factories and bring jobs back.» According to this sentence, what do some people say tariffs do? | Make shoppers pay less | Stop all trade with other countries | Close all the shops for ever | *Protect home factories and bring jobs back
